@*
 * SECTION 13: READING TEMPDATA AFTER A REDIRECT (POST-REDIRECT-GET)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * This view is the GET destination after a successful Create POST redirect.
 * TempData values set in ProductsController.Create(POST) are available here
 * because TempData survives exactly one redirect.
 *
 * HOW TEMPDATA REACHES THE VIEW:
 *   1. Create(POST) sets StatusMessage ([TempData] property) before redirecting.
 *   2. The CookieTempDataProvider writes the value to an HTTP cookie on the response.
 *   3. Browser follows the 302 → sends GET /Products/Index with the cookie.
 *   4. MVC reads the cookie, populates ITempDataDictionary before the action runs.
 *   5. [TempData] attribute filter loads TempData["StatusMessage"] into the property.
 *   6. After the action executes, the framework marks read entries for deletion.
 *   7. This view reads TempData["StatusMessage"] — still accessible in the current
 *      request's in-memory dictionary even after the attribute consumed it.
 *   8. Response commits → provider clears consumed entries from the cookie.
 *
 * NEXT REQUEST BEHAVIOUR:
 *   If the user presses F5 (refresh): GET /Index again — TempData["StatusMessage"]
 *   is now gone (deleted after the previous response). The banners do not reappear.
 *   This is the expected, correct PRG behaviour.
 *
 * PEEK vs direct read (in the view):
 *   @TempData["StatusMessage"]          → reads AND consumes (entry gone after request)
 *   @TempData.Peek("StatusMessage")     → reads WITHOUT consuming (entry stays)
 *   TempData.Keep("StatusMessage")      → call in controller to preserve across 2 requests
 *@

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Products";
}

<h1>Products</h1>

@*
 * SUCCESS BANNER — displayed once after a successful Create POST → redirect.
 * Pattern: TempData["StatusMessage"] is string msg → true only when the value
 * exists and is a non-null string. Reading it here marks it for deletion.
 * The banner will NOT appear on the next page load (PRG behaviour).
 *@
@if (TempData["StatusMessage"] is string statusMsg)
{
    <div class="alert alert-success">@statusMsg</div>
}

@*
 * ERROR BANNER — displayed once after a redirect that carried an error message.
 * In this project ErrorMessage is set if a server-side guard fails (e.g. a
 * duplicate product). Validation errors during POST use ModelState instead
 * (they don't redirect, so TempData is unnecessary for them).
 *@
@if (TempData["ErrorMessage"] is string errorMsg)
{
    <div class="alert alert-error">@errorMsg</div>
}

@*
 * INFO BANNER — check for a generic "Notice" entry left by PeekKeepDemo.
 * TempData.Keep("Notice") in PeekKeepDemo preserved it across the redirect.
 * Reading it here (once) will consume it — it won't appear on the next refresh.
 *@
@if (TempData["Notice"] is string notice)
{
    <div class="alert alert-info"><strong>Keep demo:</strong> @notice</div>
}

<p>
    This page is the <strong>GET</strong> destination in the PRG pattern.
    Use the links below to add a product or explore ViewData / ViewBag.
</p>

<p>
    <a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Create" class="btn">Add Product</a>
    &nbsp;
    <a asp-controller="Demo" asp-action="ViewDataDemo" class="btn btn-secondary">ViewData / ViewBag Demo</a>
    &nbsp;
    <a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="PeekKeepDemo" class="btn btn-secondary">Peek &amp; Keep Demo</a>
</p>

<hr />

<h2>How TempData works — at a glance</h2>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Step</th><th>Request</th><th>TempData["StatusMessage"]</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>POST /Products/Create (success)</td><td>Set to "Product saved…"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>302 Redirect issued</td><td>Persisted in cookie by provider</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>GET /Products/Index</td><td>Loaded from cookie; shown here</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>GET /Products/Index (refresh F5)</td><td><em>null</em> — consumed and deleted</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
